Let V = ⨁_{n≥0} V_{(n)} be a simple ℤ-graded vertex operator algebra over ℂ satisfying:
(i) V_{(0)} = ℂ·𝟙 (the vacuum is the unique, up to scalar, lowest-weight vector);
(ii) V_{(n)} = 0 for n < 0;
(iii) V is isomorphic to its contragredient module V′ as a V-module;
(iv) V is C_2-cofinite, i.e. dim V/C_2(V) < ∞ where C_2(V) = span{u_{-2}v : u,v ∈ V}.

Under these hypotheses, the theory of (possibly logarithmic) intertwining operators among generalized V-modules produces, via associativity and modular invariance, genus-zero and genus-one chiral correlation functions valued in multivalued analytic functions on configuration spaces of marked points on the Riemann sphere and on the torus, with logarithmic singularities permitted.

Question (two parts):
(a) Do there exist genus-zero and genus-one full (non-chiral) correlation functions for a logarithmic full conformal field theory associated with V, constructible from the genus-zero and genus-one chiral correlation functions arising from V and its module category (so that the resulting full correlation functions are single-valued on the corresponding moduli spaces and compatible with operator product expansion and the modular action)?

(b) If so, can one assemble these genus-zero and genus-one full correlation functions into a two-dimensional conformal field theory in the sense of Kontsevich and Segal — i.e. a symmetric monoidal functor from the category of Riemann surfaces with parametrized boundary to the category of (suitable topological) vector spaces, satisfying all of the Kontsevich–Segal axioms with the exception of those axioms involving unitarity (positivity of the inner product / reflection positivity)?

